description:
## Why

Current trait surface in pkix-revocation-http/src/lib.rs:36-49 is:

```rust
pub trait RevocationFetcher {
    fn fetch(&self, url: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, FetchError>;
}
```

This is sufficient for HTTP GET (CRL fetch from CDP URL) but inadequate for OCSP, which is HTTP POST with:
- a DER-encoded OCSPRequest body
- Content-Type 'application/ocsp-request'
- response Content-Type expected to be 'application/ocsp-response'

## What

Widen the trait to express request method + body + content-type. Two viable shapes:

### Option A: enum-input
```rust
pub trait RevocationFetcher {
    fn fetch(&self, req: &FetchRequest) -> Result<FetchResponse, FetchError>;
}

pub struct FetchRequest<'a> {
    pub url: &'a str,
    pub method: FetchMethod,
}
pub enum FetchMethod<'a> {
    Get,
    Post { body: &'a [u8], content_type: &'a str },
}
pub struct FetchResponse {
    pub bytes: Vec<u8>,
    pub content_type: Option<String>,
}
```

### Option B: two methods
```rust
pub trait RevocationFetcher {
    fn get(&self, url: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, FetchError>;
    fn post(&self, url: &str, body: &[u8], content_type: &str)
        -> Result<Vec<u8>, FetchError>;
}
```

Recommend **Option A** — closer to standard HTTP request shape, easier to extend with headers later (size limits, accept types, custom user-agent). Response carries content-type so callers can validate (OCSP response Content-Type sanity check).

## Acceptance

- [ ] Trait widened with structured request/response types.
- [ ] Existing FetchError variants preserved; add Status to FetchResponse if non-2xx surfacing is needed at this layer (vs. as-error).
- [ ] Plumb `V: SignatureVerifier` generic through HttpCrlFetcher<F, V> and HttpOcspFetcher<F, V> structs (currently only carry F + now_unix).
- [ ] No code changes to pkix-revocation or pkix-chain. The trait widening is fully internal to pkix-revocation-http.
- [ ] Stale 'PKIX-58m' tracking-issue references in the stub doc-comments updated to PKIX-a1yc.

## Out of scope

- Async variant (PKIX-a1yc.10).
- Header-level customization beyond content-type (future-extension).
- Size limits on response bytes (configurable, separate child if needed).

## Effort

~80 lines of trait/type definitions + doc comments + one or two compile-only test impls (e.g., a NullFetcher unit-test stub) to prove the trait shape works.
